  "theory": {
    "intro": "A handful of logarithms have standard values that come straight from the meaning of a logarithm: $\\log_{a}x$ is the power that $a$ must be raised to in order to give $x$. Because $a^{1}=a$, $a^{0}=1$, $\\frac{1}{a}=a^{-1}$ and $\\sqrt{a}=a^{\\frac{1}{2}}$, the logarithms of these numbers are simply those indices. The change-of-base rule lets you rewrite a logarithm over a base you can actually evaluate.",
    "facts": [
      "$\\log_{a}a=1$, because $a=a^{1}$.",
      "$\\log_{a}1=0$, because $1=a^{0}$.",
      "$\\log_{a}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right)=-1$, and more generally $\\log_{a}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=-\\log_{a}x$, because $\\frac{1}{x}=x^{-1}$.",
      "$\\log_{a}\\sqrt{a}=\\frac{1}{2}$, because $\\sqrt{a}=a^{\\frac{1}{2}}$.",
      "A coefficient multiplies the standard value, so $3\\log_{a}a=3\\times1=3$ and $5\\log_{a}1=5\\times0=0$.",
      "**Change of base:** $\\log_{a}b=\\frac{\\log_{n}b}{\\log_{n}a}$ for any convenient base $n$; choosing $n$ so both logs are exact gives an exact fraction."
    ]
  },
  "practice": {
